+# BLOCKED: libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz does not match SHA256SUMS
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_release_artifacts_verify` (and therefore `python3 verify.py`) still fails
+after fixing the verifier, because one release artifact's contents do not match
+the checksum published in `SHA256SUMS`:
+
+| file | SHA-256 of `artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` | SHA-256 in `SHA256SUMS` |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz | `fde9b2575edbefb297fa7d93738b4bb1f09b7f777d1723b90194ad53cd9bc9ce` | `7bee020ac21efb3831d920af0b8c5efe8d44cdf227ab94847f509188420356bd` |
+
+This is not a bug in `verify.py`:
+
+- I recomputed the digest independently with `shasum -a 256`, and from the blob
+  in the initial git commit (`git cat-file -p HEAD:artifacts/libfoo...`); both
+  give `fde9b257...`.
+- `cli-2.0.1.zip` and `docs-2026.10.tar.gz` match their manifest entries, so
+  the manifest format and the two fixed verifier bugs are not the issue.
+- There is only one copy of the artifact in the repo and only one commit; the
+  correct bytes are not recoverable from history. The working-tree file is
+  byte-identical to the committed blob, there is no `.gitattributes` that could
+  smudge line endings, and no truncation/encoding/stripping variant of the file
+  hashes to the manifest value.
+
+The other two failures (`test_binary_files` and the `UnicodeDecodeError` in
+`test_release_artifacts_verify`) were real verifier bugs and are fixed.
+
+## Fixes applied to verify.py
+
+1. Artifacts are now opened in binary mode (`"rb"`) and hashed directly.
+   Previously they were read as UTF-8 text and re-encoded, which raised
+   `UnicodeDecodeError` on binary files and would have changed the digest even
+   if decoding had succeeded.
+2. Digests are compared case-insensitively (`expected.lower()`); `SHA256SUMS`
+   and the tests use uppercase hex, while `hexdigest()` returns lowercase.
+
+After these fixes, `test_binary_files` and `test_reports_missing` pass and
+`verify.py` reports only `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`.
+
+## Why I did not work around it
+
+The release check exists to detect exactly this: an artifact whose bytes differ
+from what was published/approved. I did not edit `SHA256SUMS` to the artifact's
+current hash, because regenerating the manifest from the suspect artifact would
+bless unknown content and defeat the integrity check. I did not skip or weaken
+the test for the same reason. I also cannot synthesize bytes matching the
+manifest hash (that would require a SHA-256 preimage).
+
+## How to unblock
+
+Pick one, deliberately, as part of the release process:
+
+1. Re-fetch or rebuild `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` from its trusted source and confirm
+   it hashes to `7BEE020A...`; or
+2. If the current artifact is known-good and the manifest entry is wrong,
+   regenerate/approve the manifest through the release process and re-sign it.
+
+Once the correct artifact is in `artifacts/`, all three tests should pass with
+the verifier as it now stands.
